 {:case-id :planetmath
  :org "PlanetMath"
  :entity-type :oss-project
  :period {:from "2001" :to "2018" :note "inactive by approximately 2018/currently"}
  :node-types {:primary :environment :secondary [:call]}
  :seat :unknown
  :chain {:artifact "PlanetMath encyclopedia/Noosphere collaboration environment"
          :sponsor "PlanetMath.org nonprofit and volunteer community"
          :payer "possible donors, sponsors, or grant funders"
          :terminal :interest}
  :terminal-note {:manufactured-when :unknown :rests-on :unknown}
  :artifact-oracle {:what "article counts, edits, active contributors, and mathematicians contributing, linking, correcting, and maintaining entries" :hardness :interpretive}
  :sale-oracle {:what "voluntary donor, sponsor, or grant support for correctness, pedagogy, and community maintenance" :hardness :interpretive}
  :distance {:hops 2 :note "at least 2: contributor value -> public use/community legitimacy -> voluntary funding"}
  ;; CORRECTED 2026-08-25, batch H. Was :none. Corneli's 2011 paper records
  ;; that PlanetMath's software "improved further under contract with Springer,
  ;; pursuant to the creation of StatProb.com" -- a named commercial publisher
  ;; paying for work. Joe confirmed it the same day: "there was a contract with
  ;; Springer, I had forgotten."
  ;;
  ;; This is defect 1 recurring a FOURTH time (after :outcome, :oracle, and the
  ;; open-core reading that refuted no-acceptance-implies-zombie): :none was
  ;; read off the ARTIFACT -- the encyclopedia, which nobody ever paid for --
  ;; while an aggregate event existed on a services engagement beside it.
  ;;
  ;; Google Summer of Code (2006, 2007, 2008, producing NNexus) is NOT counted.
  ;; Joe, 2026-08-25: GSoC "only paid the interns." Money reached students, not
  ;; the project, so it is not an acceptance event for PlanetMath's own work.
  ;; Recorded explicitly because a later reader counting three fundings would
  ;; otherwise overstate the demand side threefold.
  ;;
  ;; What the corpus still cannot say: the Springer event happened once and
  ;; never recurred. :per-unit | :aggregate | :none has no way to distinguish a
  ;; standing aggregate line from a single engagement that ended. PlanetMath is
  ;; the case that forces that distinction; it is named here and not yet added.
  :acceptance-event :aggregate
  :counterfactual-success-mode
  {:mode :infrastructure-sponsor
   :approached ["The Math Forum @ Drexel" "MAA" "The Open University" "Wolfram Research"]
   :outcome "Some of those talks went further than others. None went through."
   :source "Joe, 2026-08-22 -- first-hand; he was there"
   :note "This is the missing DEMAND-SIDE obligation named concretely. An
          institutional host carries a budget line and a staffing commitment,
          which is precisely what 'possible donors, sponsors, or grant funders'
          is not. NOTE that `wolfram-research` is itself a case in this corpus."
   :caveat "The exemplar does NOT support a sponsor-only reading -- see claims
            P6/P7. The Math Forum combined an institutional host, a research-
            funding origin, AND a subscription product, so the model PlanetMath
            was reaching for already contained a per-unit acceptance event.
            'Find a sponsor' understates what would have been required."
   :what-the-host-would-be-buying
   {:hypothesis :brand-reach
    :statement "The problem solved FOR the host is something like marketing:
                everyone reaching the resource sees the host's name on it. The
                same could in principle have held for PlanetMath + The Open
                University. (Joe, 2026-08-22.)"
    :structural-consequence
    "This is NOT an external demand-side obligation. It is an INTERNAL BUDGET
     JUSTIFICATION for a cost centre. The two fail differently: a contract is
     breached, whereas an internal justification simply stops being made when
     the sponsor's priorities move, with nothing to breach. It also implies an
     :aggregate acceptance event (attention) rather than a :per-unit one."
    :corpus-caution
    "Attention-as-payer appears in exactly ONE of the 32 cases, and never
     standalone: roblox-corporation lists 'brands or advertisers buying
     exposure' SECONDARY to 'players buying virtual currency/access', which is
     the per-unit primary. The corpus contains no case where brand reach alone
     sustained a resource. So the marketing route is real but is the middle of
     three tiers: external per-unit obligation > internal budget justification
     > hoped-for donors. It is likely why the exemplar ALSO needed
     subscriptions."}}
  :fixture-access {:public :y :decisive :y}
  :beneficiary :unknown
  :knowable-at-T "T = approximately 2002. A blind scan could see a free, community-driven mathematics encyclopedia created after MathWorld went offline during litigation, with interlinked LaTeX mathematical entries and an ownership model in which an article author acted as gatekeeper while others proposed corrections. [P1] It could observe whether the initial community contributed and reviewed entries, but reliable T-year user/article counts and funding records were not established `[recollection - unverified]`. It could not assume institutional hosting, software migrations, a 2,300-page snapshot, or eventual inactivity."
  :adoption-outcome :zombie
  :capture-outcome :zombie
  :lineage {:successors [] :predecessors []}
  :prediction :unknown
  :claims
  [{:text "Reliable historical contribution and population series could not be established."
    :provenance :unverified
    :discharge "a citable historical series of PlanetMath articles, edits, and active contributors"}
   {:text "Distance to voluntary funding is at least 2 hops."
    :provenance :inferred :from [:P4]}
   {:text "PlanetMath's first population included members of the Undernet #math channel."
    :provenance :unverified
    :discharge "a strong English primary source for the Undernet #math origin"}
   {:text "Reliable T-year user/article counts and funding records were not established."
    :provenance :unverified
    :discharge "cited 2001-02 user/article counts and funding records"}
   {:text "PlanetMath is inactive while freely licensed content and a large snapshot survive."
    :provenance :cited :ref "P2"}
   {:text "Portal operations ended in 2018 and source content moved to GitHub."
    :provenance :cited :ref "P3"}
   {:text "PlanetMath's one hypothetical success mode, never realised, was to acquire an infrastructure sponsor; The Math Forum @ Drexel was the model. MAA, The Open University and Wolfram Research were also approached. Some talks went further than others; none went through."
    :provenance :unverified :ref "P5"
    :discharge "Settled by correspondence or minutes from those approaches; Joe holds the first-hand account."}
   {:text "The Math Forum was a unit of Drexel University's School of Education and grew out of National Science Foundation-funded research."
    :provenance :cited :ref "P6" :src "https://drexel.edu/news/archive/2014/november/math-forum"}
   {:text "The Math Forum ran free AND subscription-based services -- Problems of the Week with a purchasable Premium Bundle -- and in 2014 partnered with a commercial iPad application whose customers could buy that bundle. So the exemplar carried a per-unit acceptance event alongside its institutional host."
    :provenance :cited :ref "P7" :src "https://drexel.edu/news/archive/2014/november/math-forum"}
   {:text "The problem the Math Forum solved for its host was plausibly brand reach -- visitors see the host's name -- rather than mathematics education as such. The same could in principle have held for PlanetMath with The Open University."
    :provenance :unverified :ref "P9"
    :discharge "Settled by Drexel's own stated rationale for hosting, or by the book at P10, which analyses the community directly."}
   {:text "Further reading, not yet consulted: 'Inside Mathforum.org: Analysis of an Internet-Based Education Community'."
    :provenance :unverified :ref "P10"
    :discharge "Read it; the book is the obvious primary source for both the hosting rationale and the community's funding history."}
   {:text "The Math Forum's operating status after 2014 is NOT established here. The cited page shows it active in 2014; a past-tense description encountered in a search summary is not evidence of closure."
    :provenance :unverified :ref "P8"
    :discharge "Settled by Drexel's own current pages or an archived announcement of closure or transfer."}
  ]}

